# Folder shapes design

Which files and folders a project folder may hold, and what Fix changes in a project folder.
A project folder is the folder of a registered project of any kind (see ControllersDesign).

## Desks

A D models a 'desk': an operating system with the file systems it mounts.
```
D ::= ids; folders
folder ::= id{Es}
E ::= s -> e
e ::= id | file | other
p ::= id s1..sn
z ::= {Zs}
Z ::= s -> z | s -> file
t ::= e | z
v ::= visible | hidden
```
- In a D, the ids of the folders are distinct.
- In a D, every id of the ids, and every id of an E of the folders, is the id of a folder of the folders.
- In a folder, the s of the Es are distinct.
- In a z, the s of the Zs are distinct.

`s`, `file` and `other` are atoms; a `file` has a content and a time. `len(s)` is a natural number.
`isHidden(s)`, `isZip(s)`, `isZipOf(s1, s2)`, `folderNameOk(s)`, `fileNameOk(s)`, `hiddenNameOk(s)`, `okTogether(s1, s2)` and `timeOk(file)` are predicates, and `zip(file)` is a z or undefined.
The implementation notes define them for each desk.
The ids of a D are the roots.
```
at(D, id)= id                 with D= _ id _; _
at(D, p s)= e                 with at(D, p)= id, id{_ s -> e _} in D
at(D, p s)= t                 with at(D, p)= file, zip(file)= {_ s -> t _}
at(D, p s)= t                 with at(D, p)= {_ s -> t _}
root(D, id s1..sn)            iff  n= 0, D= _ id _; _
add(D, p s, e)= ids; folders id{Es s -> e} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
                          at(D, p s) undefined
create(D, p s, file)= add(D, p s, file)
makeFolder(D, p s)= add(ids; folders id{}, p s, id)
                     with D= ids; folders
                          id is not the id of a folder of D
move(D, p1 s1, p2 s2)= add(delete(D, p1 s1), p2 s2, at(D, p1 s1))
delete(D, p s)= ids; folders id{Es Es'} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es s -> _ Es'} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
rename(D, p s, s')= ids; folders id{Es s' -> e Es'} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es s -> e Es'} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
                          at(D, p s') undefined
write(D, p s, file')= ids; folders id{Es s -> file' Es'} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es s -> file Es'} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
```
When `at(D, p)` is a file f, or a z inside zip(f), `add`, `delete`, `rename` and `write` on `p s` change zip(f), and f is written again from the changed zip(f), with the current time.
A `move` of a z to a folder of D adds a folder with a new id, holding the Zs of that z, each z among them moved in the same way.
A 'checkable folder property' is a predicate on Ds and paths that never compares two ids.
```
names(D, id)= Es              with id{Es} in D
names(D, {Zs})= Zs
names(D, file)= names(D, zip(file))
valid(D, p)         iff  at(D, p)= id, names(D, id) not empty, ok(D, names(D, id), visible, 0, 0)
ok(D, s1 -> t1 .. sk -> tk, v, l, d)
                    iff  forall i, j in 1..k with i != j: okTogether(si, sj)
                         forall i, j in 1..k with i != j: (v= visible, not isHidden(si), isZip(si)) implies (not isZipOf(si, sj))
                         forall i in 1..k: okEntry(D, si, ti, v, l+len(si), d)
okEntry(D, s, t, v, L, d)
                    iff  one of these holds:
                         v= visible, not isHidden(s), L <= 200, (t= id or t= z), folderNameOk(s),
                           names(D, t) not empty, ok(D, names(D, t), visible, L+1, d)
                         v= visible, not isHidden(s), L <= 200, t= file, fileNameOk(s), (d= 0 implies timeOk(t)), not isZip(s)
                         v= visible, not isHidden(s), L <= 200, t= file, fileNameOk(s), (d= 0 implies timeOk(t)), isZip(s), d <= 64,
                           zip(t)= z, names(D, z) not empty, ok(D, names(D, z), visible, L-3, d+1)
                         (v= hidden or isHidden(s)), L <= 250, t= file, hiddenNameOk(s)
                         (v= hidden or isHidden(s)), L <= 250, (t= id or t= z), hiddenNameOk(s),
                           (v= visible implies names(D, t) not empty), ok(D, names(D, t), hidden, L+1, d)
same(D, p1, p2)     iff  at(D, p1)= id1, at(D, p2)= id2,
                         exists s, file: at(D, p1 s) undefined, at(D, p2 s) undefined, at(create(D, p1 s, file), p2 s)= file
inside(D, p1, p2)   iff  at(D, p1)= id1, at(D, p2)= id2, exists s1..sn with n > 0: same(D, p2 s1..sn, p1)
```

## Entries

An 'entry' is `at(D, p s1..sn)`, with p the project folder and n > 0, when every file that `at` goes through is a 'zip'.
A 'zip' is a 'visible' file whose name s has `isZip(s)`.
The 'path' of an 'entry' is the names from the project folder to the 'entry', joined with `/`, where each 'zip' `x.zip` on the way counts as `x`: the file `a.fear` of `lib/x.zip` has the 'path' `lib/x/a.fear`.
An 'entry' is 'hidden' when `isHidden` holds for a name of the 'path' of the 'entry'.
Any other 'entry' is 'visible'.

## Shape rules

A project folder p is 'well shaped' when `valid(D, p)` holds; the 'shape rules' are the premises of `valid`, `ok` and `okEntry`.
The 'problem' (see Project states in ControllersDesign) of a project whose project folder is not 'well shaped' names one broken 'shape rule' and the 'path' breaking that 'shape rule'.
The code checking the 'shape rules' is `Coordinator/src/realSourceOracle`.
The error on a too long 'path' says 200 characters, also for a 'hidden' 'entry'.

## Names

A 'valid segment' holds only lowercase letters (`a` to `z`), digits (`0` to `9`) and `_`, starts with a letter or `_`, and is not `con`, `prn`, `aux`, `nul`, `com1` to `com9` or `lpt1` to `lpt9`.
A 'valid file name' is one of:
- a 'valid segment', a dot and an extension of 1 to 16 lowercase letters or digits (`0` to `9`);
- a 'valid segment', a dot and one of `css.map`, `d.ts`, `d.ts.map`, `js.map`, `min.css`, `min.js`, `tar.bz2`, `tar.gz`, `tar.lz`, `tar.lz4`, `tar.xz`, `tar.zst`.

A 'hidden name':
- does not end with a dot or a space;
- holds no control character, no surrogate without its pair, and none of `<`, `>`, `:`, `"`, `/`, `\`, `|`, `?`, `*`;
- before the first dot, in lowercase, is not `con`, `prn`, `aux`, `nul`, `conin$`, `conout$`, `com1` to `com9`, `lpt1` to `lpt9`, or `com` or `lpt` followed by U+00B9, U+00B2 or U+00B3;
- is at most 255 bytes long in UTF-8.

A 'zip file' is a file in the zip format whose entries are stored or deflated, and not encrypted.
The 'stored names' of a 'zip file' are the names its central directory stores. The 'stored names' are 'good' when:
- every name is valid UTF-8, holds no NUL character, and, without one `/` at its end, holds no segment that is empty, `.` or `..`;
- no name is stored twice, comparing the names without one `/` at their end;
- no name starts with the name of a file followed by `/`.

## Fix

Fix is an operation of the manager on one project (`Fix <name>` in ControllersDesign).

A 'junk file' is one of:
- a file named `.DS_Store`;
- a file whose name starts with `._`;
- a file named `Icon` followed by a carriage return;
- a file named `Thumbs.db`, `ehthumbs.db`, `ehthumbs_vista.db` or `desktop.ini`, in any mix of uppercase and lowercase;
- a file whose name ends with `:Zone.Identifier`;
- a file named `.directory`;
- a file whose name ends with `~`;
- a file whose name starts with `~$`;
- a file whose name starts with `.~lock.` and ends with `#`;
- a file whose name starts with `#` and ends with `#`;
- a file whose name starts with `.` and ends with `.swp`.
A 'junk folder' is a folder named `__MACOSX` or `.AppleDouble`, or a folder whose name starts with `.Trash-`.

The 'fixed segment' of a text is that text in lowercase, with every sequence of characters other than `a` to `z`, `0` to `9` and `_` turned into one `_`, and with a leading `p` when the result is not a 'valid segment'.
The 'guessed extension' of a file is:
- `png` when the content of the file starts with the bytes `89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A`;
- `jpg` when the content of the file starts with the bytes `FF D8 FF`;
- `gif` when the content of the file starts with the text `GIF87a` or `GIF89a`;
- `txt` when the content of the file is UTF-8 text holding no control character other than tab, line feed and carriage return; a file with no content is such a file;
- `bin` otherwise.
The 'fixed name' of a folder is the 'fixed segment' of the name of the folder.
The 'fixed name' of a file is the 'fixed segment' of the stem of the file, a dot and the extension of the file, where the stem is the name without the dot and the extension, and the extension is:
- `css.map`, `d.ts`, `d.ts.map`, `js.map`, `min.css`, `min.js`, `tar.bz2`, `tar.gz`, `tar.lz`, `tar.lz4`, `tar.xz` or `tar.zst`, when the name in lowercase ends with a dot and that extension;
- otherwise the end of the name after the last dot, in lowercase, when that end is 1 to 16 letters (`a` to `z`, `A` to `Z`) or digits (`0` to `9`);
- otherwise the 'guessed extension' of the file; the stem is then the whole name.
The 'fixed name' number k of an entry is its 'fixed name' with k added at the end of the 'fixed segment'.
Two names 'clash' when the two names are equal after lowercasing and after removing an ending `.zip`.

The 'cleaned file' of a file is the file, with the current time, whose content is the result of the run of `StrCleaners.softText` (see TextCleanersDesign) on the UTF-8 bytes of the text of the file, after turning U+2018 and U+2019 into `'`, U+201C and U+201D into `"`, and U+2013, U+2014 and U+2212 into `-`.
The text of the file is the UTF-16 text when the file starts with a UTF-16 byte order mark (the bytes `FF FE` or `FE FF`), the UTF-8 text when the bytes of the file are UTF-8, and the windows-1252 text otherwise.
The 'cleaning lines' of a file are `Removed the byte order mark of <path>`, `Fixed the line endings of <path>` and `Cleaned <k> characters of <path>` (`Cleaned 1 character of <path>` when `<k>` is 1): the first when the text starts with a byte order mark, the second when the line endings change, the third when `<k>` is more than 0; `<k>` is the number of characters of the text, after the byte order mark is removed and the line endings are fixed, other than the line feed and U+0020 to U+007E.

The 'restored names' of a 'zip file' are its 'stored names' where every `\` becomes `/`, and each name is then its segments that are neither empty nor `.`, joined with `/`, with a `/` at the end when the stored name ends with `/`; a name with no such segment is no longer stored.
A 'zip file' written from a z keeps, for each file that Fix does not change, its name, its content, its time and its place in the order of the zip.

In the pseudocode, `say <line>` adds a line at the end of the Output of the project, and `end <line>` adds a line and ends Fix.
`<path>` is the 'path' of the 'entry' `p s`.
The 'fix folder' of a Fix on a project folder named `<f>` is the first of `<f>_fix`, `<f>_fix2`, `<f>_fix3`, ... with nothing at that name in the folder holding the project folder when the Fix starts.
```
fix(p)
  empty the Output
  fixFolder(p, visible, 0, 0)
  if the Output is empty: say `The project "<name>" needs no fix.`, with <name> the name of the project

fixFolder(p, v, l, d)
  for each s of names(D, at(D, p)) when fixFolder starts, in the order of the s: fixEntry(p, s, v, l, d)

fixEntry(p, s, v, l, d)
  t= at(D, p s)
  v'= hidden if v= hidden or isHidden(s), visible otherwise
  if (t= file and a file named s is a 'junk file') or ((t= id or t= z) and a folder named s is a 'junk folder'):
    D= delete(D, p s); say `Deleted <path>`; return
  if t= other: away(p s); return
  if v'= visible and (((t= id or t= z) and not folderNameOk(s)) or (t= file and not fileNameOk(s))
                      or (isZip(s) and isZipOf(s, s'') for an s'' of names(D, at(D, p)))):
    s'= the first of the 'fixed name' of p s, its 'fixed name' number 2, number 3, ...,
        with folderNameOk(s') when t= id or t= z, with fileNameOk(s') when t= file, clashing with no other s of names(D, at(D, p))
    D= rename(D, p s, s'); say `Renamed <path> to <s'>`; s= s'
  L= l+len(s)
  if (v'= hidden and not hiddenNameOk(s)) or (v'= visible and L > 200) or L > 250
     or not okTogether(s'', s) for an s'' before s in names(D, at(D, p)):
    away(p s); return
  if v'= visible and t= file and s ends with `.fear` and the 'cleaned file' of t is not t:
    D= write(D, p s, the 'cleaned file' of t); say each of the 'cleaning lines' of t
  t= at(D, p s)
  if t= file and d= 0 and not timeOk(t):
    D= write(D, p s, t with the current time); say `Reset the time of <path>`
  if t= id or t= z:
    fixFolder(p s, v', L+1, d)
    if v= visible and names(D, at(D, p s)) is empty: D= delete(D, p s); say `Deleted the empty folder <path>`
  if t= file and v'= visible and isZip(s):
    fixZip(p s, L-3, d+1)

fixZip(p s, l, d)
  t= at(D, p s), with the 'restored names' in place of its 'stored names'
  if zip(t) is undefined or d > 65: away(p s); return
  if the 'restored names' differ from the 'stored names': D= write(D, p s, t with the current time); say `Fixed the stored names of <path>`
  fixFolder(p s, visible, l, d)
  if zip(at(D, p s)) holds no Z: D= delete(D, p s); say `Deleted the empty zip <path>`

away(p s)
  q= the 'fix folder'
  if a name of the 'path' of p s below q names a file or an other: end `file already exists:` and the path on the disk of that name
  D= makeFolder of q, when q is missing, and of each folder of the 'path' of p s below q that is missing
  D= move(D, p s, the 'path' of p s below q)
  say `Moved <path> to <q>/<path>`, with <q> the path on the disk of q
```
When Fix does not `end`, `valid(D, p)` holds after Fix, unless the project folder holds no entry.
A `delete`, a `rename`, a `write`, a `makeFolder` or a `move` that the desk refuses ends Fix with `end` and the error of the desk and the path on the disk that the desk refuses.
A `rename` to a name that the desk takes as the name of another 'entry' is `end` `file already exists:` and the path on the disk of that name.

## Implementation notes

The sections below say, for each desk, which operations of the desk correspond to which operations on a D.
An operation of a desk is a program run in a shell: `sh` on Linux and macOS, PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`) on Windows.
Each correspondence holds while no other program changes the folders that the operation reads or writes.
`<disk path>` is the path on the disk corresponding to p.

An id corresponds to a folder of the desk; no program prints an id.
A mount point corresponds to a root id: one of the ids of a D.
The pair of the device number and the inode number that a desk gives for a folder does not correspond to an id.
Two mount points of one shared folder of the network give that folder two different device numbers, so two different pairs, while `same` holds for the two paths.

### Linux

- An s corresponds to 1 or more bytes, none of them `0x00` or `0x2F` (`/`), other than `.` and `..`.
- `len(s)` is the number of bytes of s.
- `isHidden(s)`: the first byte of s is `0x2E` (`.`).
- `isZip(s)`: the last four bytes of s are the ASCII bytes of `.zip`.
- `isZipOf(s1, s2)`: s1 is the bytes of s2 followed by the ASCII bytes of `.zip`.
- `folderNameOk(s)`: s is the ASCII bytes of a 'valid segment' (see Names).
- `fileNameOk(s)`: s is the ASCII bytes of a 'valid file name' (see Names).
- `hiddenNameOk(s)`: s is valid UTF-8, and the text of s is a 'hidden name' (see Names).
- `okTogether(s1, s2)`: s1 and s2 are not both valid UTF-8 with texts equal after Unicode normalization (NFC) and Unicode simple case folding.
- `timeOk(file)`: the time of the file is after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z and at most 5 seconds after the current time. The time of a file of the folders is the last modified time, the number of seconds that the program `stat`, run as `stat -c %Y <disk path>`, prints; the time of a file of a z is the time of the 'zip file' holding that z.
- `zip(file)`: the tree of the 'stored names' of the 'zip file' (see Names), split at `/`, each segment as its UTF-8 bytes, where a name ending with `/` is a z; undefined when the file is not a 'zip file', or its 'stored names' are not 'good'.
- The mount points are the paths in the fifth field of each line of the file `/proc/self/mountinfo`, with each escape `\ooo` (three octal digits) read as the byte it stands for. `/` is one of them.
- A path made of a mount point `m` followed by `/s1`, ..., `/sn`, given to a program, corresponds to the p `id s1..sn`, where `m` is the longest mount point the path starts with, comparing whole names, and `id` is the root id corresponding to `m`.
  This holds when each `si` is one of the names that the program `find` prints for the path made of `m` followed by `/s1`, ..., `/si-1`, as below, and `stat` prints `directory` for each of the paths ending at s1, ..., sn-1.
  For example, with the mount points `/` and `/home`, the path `/home/user/proj` corresponds to `id user proj`, with `id` corresponding to `/home`.
- The program `find`, run as `find <disk path> -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -printf '%f\0'`, prints the s of the Es of the folder `at(D, p)`, each followed by the byte `0x00`.
- The program `stat`, run as `stat -c %F <disk path>`:
  - prints `directory` when `at(D, p)` is an id;
  - prints `regular file` or `regular empty file` when `at(D, p)` is a file;
  - prints `symbolic link`, `fifo`, `socket`, `character special file`, `block special file`, or any other text, when `at(D, p)` is an other;
  - fails with `No such file or directory` when `at(D, p)` is undefined.
- The shell command `[ ! -e <disk path>/s ] && [ ! -L <disk path>/s ] && : > <disk path>/s` creates the file `<disk path>/s`, and fails when an entry of that name exists; it corresponds to `create(D, p s, file)`.
- The program `rm`, run as `rm -r <disk path>/s`, corresponds to `delete(D, p s)` when no mount point starts with `<disk path>/s/`; when a mount point starts with `<disk path>/s/`, the desk refuses `delete(D, p s)`.
- The program `mkdir`, run as `mkdir <disk path>/s`, corresponds to `makeFolder(D, p s)`.
- The shell command `[ ! -L <disk path2>/s2 ] && [ ! -e <disk path2>/s2 ] && mv -T <disk path1>/s1 <disk path2>/s2` corresponds to `move(D, p1 s1, p2 s2)`.
- The shell command `[ ! -L <disk path>/s' ] && { [ ! -e <disk path>/s' ] || [ <disk path>/s -ef <disk path>/s' ]; } && mv -T <disk path>/s <disk path>/<n> && mv -T <disk path>/<n> <disk path>/s'`, with `<n>` a name at which no entry is, corresponds to `rename(D, p s, s')`: the two steps also rename an entry whose new name differs only in the case of the letters on a file system ignoring case.
- Writing the bytes of `file'` in `<disk path>/s`, then running the program `touch` as `touch -m -d @<seconds> <disk path>/s` with the time of `file'`, corresponds to `write(D, p s, file')`.
- The order of the s: the first byte that differs decides, and a shorter s comes before the longer s it starts.
- The 'junk file', 'junk folder', 'fixed name' and 'clash' rules read s as text: the UTF-8 text of s, where each byte that is not part of valid UTF-8 is a character other than `a` to `z`, digits and `_`.

### macOS

- An s corresponds to bytes, as on Linux.
- `len(s)` is the number of bytes of s, as on Linux.
- `isHidden(s)`, `isZip(s)`, `isZipOf(s1, s2)`, `folderNameOk(s)`, `fileNameOk(s)`, `hiddenNameOk(s)`, `okTogether(s1, s2)` and `zip(file)`: as on Linux.
- `timeOk(file)`: as on Linux, with the program `stat` run as `stat -f %m <disk path>`.
- The mount points are the paths that the program `mount`, run with no argument, prints: one line for each mount point, with the path between ` on ` and the next ` (`. `/` is one of them.
- A path given to a program corresponds to a p as on Linux.
- The program `find`, run as `find <disk path> -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -print0`, prints `<disk path>/` followed by each s of the Es of the folder `at(D, p)`, each followed by the byte `0x00`.
- The program `stat`, run as `stat -f %HT <disk path>`:
  - prints `Directory` when `at(D, p)` is an id;
  - prints `Regular File` when `at(D, p)` is a file;
  - prints `Symbolic Link`, `FIFO`, `Socket`, `Character Device`, `Block Device`, `Whiteout`, or any other text, when `at(D, p)` is an other;
  - fails with `No such file or directory` when `at(D, p)` is undefined.
- `create`, `makeFolder`, `move`, `rename`, the order of the s and the reading of s as text: as on Linux.
- The program `rm`, run as `rm -r <disk path>/s`, corresponds to `delete(D, p s)` as on Linux.
- Writing the bytes of `file'` in `<disk path>/s`, then running the program `touch` as `touch -m -d <YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:SSZ> <disk path>/s` with the time of `file'`, corresponds to `write(D, p s, file')`.

### Windows

- An s corresponds to 1 or more 16 bit units, none of them `0x0000`, `0x002F` (`/`) or `0x005C` (`\`), other than `.` and `..`.
- `len(s)` is the number of 16 bit units of s.
- `isHidden(s)`: the first unit of s is `0x002E` (`.`).
- `isZip(s)`: the last four units of s are the units of `.zip`.
- `isZipOf(s1, s2)`: s1 is the units of s2 followed by the units of `.zip`.
- `folderNameOk(s)`: s is the units of a 'valid segment' (see Names).
- `fileNameOk(s)`: s is the units of a 'valid file name' (see Names).
- `hiddenNameOk(s)`: the units of s, read as UTF-16, are a 'hidden name' (see Names).
- `okTogether(s1, s2)`: the texts of s1 and s2, read as UTF-16, are not equal after Unicode normalization (NFC) and Unicode simple case folding.
- `timeOk(file)`: as on Linux, where the last modified time is the value `LastWriteTimeUtc` of `Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path> -Force`.
- `zip(file)`: as on Linux, with each segment as the 16 bit units of its text.
- The mount points are the paths that the program `mountvol`, run with no argument, prints for each volume, like `C:\` and `C:\mnt\usb\`, the root `X:\` of each drive whose name is one letter in `Get-PSDrive -PSProvider FileSystem`, also mapped network drives and `subst` drives, and every shared folder of the network, like `\\server\share\`.
- A path made of a mount point `m` followed by `s1\`, ..., `sn`, given to a program, corresponds to a p as on Linux, with `\` in place of `/`, and with the command `Get-ChildItem` below in place of `find`.
  For example, with the mount points `C:\` and `C:\mnt\usb\`, the path `C:\mnt\usb\docs\a` corresponds to `id docs a`, with `id` corresponding to `C:\mnt\usb\`.
- The PowerShell command `Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath <disk path> -Force -Name` prints the s of the Es of the folder `at(D, p)`, one for each line.
- The PowerShell command `(Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path> -Force).Attributes` prints the attributes of the entry. When the attributes hold `ReparsePoint`, the program `fsutil`, run as `fsutil reparsepoint query <disk path>`, prints the tag and the target of the entry.
  - `at(D, p)` is an other when the tag is `0xA000000C` (a symbolic link), `0xA000001D` (a symbolic link of WSL), `0x8000001B` (an app execution alias), or `0xA0000003` with a target other than `\??\Volume{<guid>}\` (a junction).
  - Otherwise `at(D, p)` is an id when the attributes hold `Directory`, and a file when they do not: a folder where a volume is mounted, a OneDrive placeholder, a compressed file and a deduplicated file are folders and files.
  - `at(D, p)` is undefined when `Get-Item` fails because no entry is at `<disk path>`.
- The PowerShell command `New-Item -Path <disk path>\s -ItemType File` creates the file `<disk path>\s`, and fails when an entry of that name exists; it corresponds to `create(D, p s, file)`.
- The PowerShell command `Remove-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\s -Recurse -Force` corresponds to `delete(D, p s)`: PowerShell 7 deletes a symbolic link or a junction inside `<disk path>\s` without following it.
- The PowerShell command `New-Item -Path <disk path>\s -ItemType Directory` corresponds to `makeFolder(D, p s)`.
- The PowerShell command `if (Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path2>\s2 -Force -ErrorAction Ignore) { throw }; Move-Item -LiteralPath <disk path1>\s1 -Destination <disk path2>\s2` corresponds to `move(D, p1 s1, p2 s2)`.
- The PowerShell command `$e= Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\s' -Force -ErrorAction Ignore; if ($e -and $e.Name -cne 's') { throw }; Rename-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\s -NewName <n>; Rename-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\<n> -NewName s'`, with `<n>` a name at which no entry is, corresponds to `rename(D, p s, s')`.
- The PowerShell command `Set-Content -LiteralPath <disk path>\s -AsByteStream -Force -Value <bytes>`, then `(Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\s -Force).LastWriteTimeUtc= <time>`, with the bytes and the time of `file'`, corresponds to `write(D, p s, file')`.
- The order of the s: the first unit that differs decides, and a shorter s comes before the longer s it starts.
- The 'junk file', 'junk folder', 'fixed name' and 'clash' rules read s as text: the UTF-16 text of s, where each surrogate without its pair is a character other than `a` to `z`, digits and `_`.
